  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 108 AM CDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nAn elongated 1020 mb surface high will build across the Upper \nMississippi Valley today yielding another pleasant early fall\nday. Northwesterly breezes will develop towards midday and into\nthis afternoon, and have inched gusts up a bit towards 20 mph \nalong the northeastern flanks of the high where progged deeper \nmixing is forecast. Have continued to generally undercut NBM \ndewpoints given the anticipated mixing today which yields \nafternoon RH values into the 25 to 35 percent range. Winds will \nthen trend light/variable this evening as the surface high \ntranslates overhead.\n\nA broad region of low-level warm advection will develop and \nsubsequently intensify across Iowa late tonight before spilling \neastward across northern Illinois through Friday morning. Moist \nupglide impinging upon the northeastern terminus of a zone of \nmodestly steep mid-level lapse rates will likely yield an\nexpansion of elevated showers and some thunderstorms. As is \ntypical with these types of set ups, the location of the main \nmoist/unstable axis remains a bit unclear. That said, global \nguidance has remained fairly consistent, depicting the main zone\nof mid-level saturation lifting across northern Illinois \nthrough the afternoon. Will show scattered showers and isolated \nthunderstorms in the weather grids during this period, and \nsuspect PoPs may have to be boosted further as hires guidance \nbetter latches onto the salient forcing mechanisms. While the \nsevere weather threat is low given a dearth of instability, a \ncorridor of 30-40 kt effective bulk shear is forecast to develop\nas a 40-50 kt 500 mb jet max scoots overhead. Some recent CAMs \nhave an interesting presentation to a convective conglomeration \npushing east of the Mississippi River towards midday, although \nthis would likely weaken with time as it outpaces the main \ninstability reservoir.\n\nAny lingering activity will depart the region late Friday \nafternoon/early evening. Saturday continues to look dry,\nsomewhat breezy, and warm, with initial high cirrus streaming \nnorthward through the afternoon. The remnants of Isaias \ninteracting with a northward-advancing trough will result in a \nchance for showers across parts of our area Saturday night into \nSunday morning. With such a dry antecedent airmass in place, \ncombined with northern Illinois remaining on the western extent \nof the advancing jet stream, suggests that precipitation \ncoverage and amounts may end up being fairly limited in the \nregion with max upper jet support focusing across the Ohio River\nValley (barring any westward jog to the main synoptic scale \nfeatures). \n\nConditions currently look dry to start next week, but an\nadditional cold front will bring the next chance for a brief \nperiod of showers to the region Monday night into Tuesday before\nhigh pressure builds back across the area mid week. \n\nCarlaw",
